    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "yeah i'll check the node metrics. but honestly everything looks pretty stable on the dashboard when i check it manually. it's just these random, weird spikes that happen for like 30 seconds and then vanish. it's driving the team crazy because it feels so random and almost intentional, like something is actively messing with the connections just to trip us up. \n\nis there any way to run a more granular trace on the network level? maybe something that can catch these momentary drops as they happen without me having to stare at a terminal all day?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To capture those fleeting network issues, you should implement continuous, low-overhead network tracing like `tcpdump` or Wireshark directly on the build node during the test execution. Configure this trace to capture and save packets only when high error rates or connection resets are logged in your pipeline output. Alternatively, enabling verbose network debugging within your testing framework itself can sometimes expose the underlying socket errors before a hard timeout occurs.",
      "source": "target",
